# What belongs in this repository is written down in ADR-007
# (docs/decisions/007-what-goes-into-git.md, kept out of git — see below)
# and, as an event, in the journal: `kadence decision list`.

# ── Dependencies ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
node_modules/
.npm/
.pnpm-store/

# ── Build output ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# dist/ is published to npm, not committed: `npm run build` regenerates it
# and a checked-in bundle conflicts on every merge.
dist/
*.tsbuildinfo
# `npm pack` writes the tarball into the repository root.
*.tgz

# ── Test and coverage artefacts ───────────────────────────────────────
coverage/
*.lcov
.vitest/

# ── Logs and crash reports ────────────────────────────────────────────
*.log
npm-debug.log*
yarn-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*

# ── Local environment and credentials ─────────────────────────────────
# Secrets never enter the journal. An .env.example may be committed.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
# This package is published to npm, so a local .npmrc holds an auth token
# (`//registry.npmjs.org/:_authToken=`). It is per developer and never shared.
.npmrc

# ── Editors and OS ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
.DS_Store
._*
.Spotlight-V100
.Trashes
Thumbs.db
.idea/
.vscode/
*.swp
*~

# ── Developer tooling — local to each machine, not to the project ─────
# Agent skills, caches, hooks and indexes are reinstalled per developer.
# The committed contract for agents is AGENTS.md and .kadence/README.md,
# and both are generated by the product itself (`kadence init`), so no
# vendor's configuration needs to be in here.
.claude/
.serena/
.cursor/
.aider*

# ── kadence's own board ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# .kadence/events/ IS the data and is committed on purpose — that is the
# whole product. state.json is a derived snapshot, rebuilt from events.
.kadence/state.json
.kadence/*.lock

# ── The landing page ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
# site/ is drafted here but lives in a repository of its own — ADR-008.
# The files stay on disk; they do not enter this product's history.
site/

# ── docs/ — the working papers, not the product ───────────────────────
# Decided 2026-09-23. This repository publishes what a user of kadence
# needs: the binary's source, its tests, the agent contract, and the
# journal the product keeps about itself. docs/ is the other thing — the
# owner's working papers. Three reasons, in order of weight:
#
#   1. Some of it is about people. A named list of developers with
#      outreach statuses, the messages written to them, a private
#      conversation quoted in full, and the owner's own hours and
#      employer. None of them agreed to be published.
#   2. Most of the rest is business-internal: strategy, go-to-market,
#      kill criteria, pricing thinking. A reader of the product gains
#      nothing from it.
#   3. What is left — ADRs, design notes — is genuinely useful, but
#      splitting the directory would leave dangling links in every
#      document that stays. The honest cut is the whole directory.
#
# The files stay on disk and the work continues in them, exactly as with
# site/. The reasoning that a reader of this repository still needs is in
# the journal, which is committed: `kadence decision list` and DEC-19,
# DEC-20 in particular.
docs/

# ── The owner's working papers ────────────────────────────────────────
# Decided 2026-09-23, same reasoning as docs/ above: this repository is the
# product, not the desk it was built at. Every one of these stays on disk.
#
#   tasks/    milestone plans and the execution log. What shipped is in
#             CHANGELOG.md, which a user reads; the plans behind it are the
#             owner's, and tasks/plan-to-1.0.md carries go-to-market and
#             gate dates that mean nothing to a reader of the code.
#   assets/   logo and lockups. They belong to the site's repository
#             (ADR-008); nothing in this one renders them.
#   SPEC.md   the original specification: modules, acceptance criteria,
#             milestones. Written for the build, in Ukrainian, and long
#             since overtaken by the code and CHANGELOG.md. What a reader
#             needs from it — the contract — is `kadence schema --json`.
#   scripts/kadence.mjs
#             a convenience wrapper that rebuilds when src/ is newer and
#             then runs dist/cli.js. `npm run build && node dist/cli.js` is
#             the same thing in two commands, so it is one developer's
#             habit, not part of the project.
tasks/
assets/
SPEC.md
scripts/kadence.mjs

# The other three scripts are NOT ignored, because something committed needs
# each one, and a repository whose CI is red on a fresh clone makes a worse
# first impression than a few spare kilobytes:
#   scripts/build.mjs      npm run build, CI, and the test globalSetup
#   scripts/reference.mjs  npm run reference, release.yml, and it ships
#                          inside the npm package (package.json "files")
#   scripts/north-star.mjs test/north-star.test.ts spawns it
#
# packages/github is not ignored either: @kadence/github is published, its
# source is what a user of it can audit, test/github-package.test.ts imports
# it, and ci.yml gates it.
#
# Nothing here is caught by `npm test`, because `git rm --cached` leaves every
# file on disk. The check that does catch it:
#   git archive $(git write-tree) | tar -x -C /tmp/clone && cd /tmp/clone \
#     && npm ci && node scripts/build.mjs && npx vitest run
